            "text": "Finds the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of a symmetric matrix: the directions it only stretches, without turning, and how much it stretches along each.\n\nIts most common use is principal component analysis. Feed it the covariance matrix of some data (t.cov) and the eigenvectors are the data's principal axes - the direction it varies most along, then the next most at right angles to that, and so on - and the eigenvalues say how much variance lies along each. The first axis of a cloud of hand positions is the main direction of the gesture; the first few axes of many joints are the main ways the body moves together.\n\nUnlike t.linalg.eig, which takes any square matrix, it assumes symmetry, and so its answers are always real numbers and its axes always at right angles. A stack of matrices is analysed in one step. The Mac's GPU has no eigh, so there it runs on the cpu and the answers are moved back.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.linalg.eigh\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nmatrix:\nA square, symmetric matrix (equal to its own transpose), such as a covariance or correlation matrix, or a stack of them. Only its lower triangle is read, so a matrix that is not symmetric gives the answer for a different matrix. Every input is analysed and sent.\n\norder:\nAn option, under the node: largest first (the default here, the principal axis first) or smallest first (the order torch itself gives).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\neigenvalues:\nHow much the matrix stretches along each eigenvector.\n\neigenvectors:\nOne eigenvector per COLUMN, in the same order as the eigenvalues, each of length 1. Sent first. The sign of each is arbitrary: (0.71, 0.71) and (-0.71, -0.71) are the same axis.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nSix points lying close to the diagonal line y = x. Their covariance, analysed: the first eigenvalue (6.94) is hundreds of times the second (0.02), and the first eigenvector, the first column, is about (-0.71, -0.71) - the diagonal, the direction the points run along (its sign is arbitrary: (0.71, 0.71) is the same axis). The second axis, at right angles, holds almost nothing: the points barely stray from the line.\n\nRELATED:\nt.cov makes the covariance matrix to feed it. t.linalg.svd does the same job for any matrix, symmetric or not. t.linalg.eig takes any square matrix; t.linalg.pca_low_rank does a whole PCA in one step. np.linalg.eigh is the numpy version."
        },
